{#
  Histogram-bucket trim for the two control-plane jobs, invoked at COLUMN 0 (it carries its own
  8-space indent, matching `relabel_configs:` in the job blocks below).

  MEASURED, not guessed. Adding the apiserver and kubelet jobs took this Prometheus from ~83k
  head series to 183,150, and 69,022 of the ~100k added were `_bucket` series from twelve
  families — apiserver_request_duration, etcd_request_duration, apiserver_request_sli_duration,
  request_body_size, watch_list_duration, response_sizes, watch_cache_read_wait,
  scheduler_plugin_execution, both workqueue duration families, watch_events_sizes and
  storage_operation_duration.

  That matters because the cap here is BYTES, not series: retention.size is 3GB against a 5Gi
  PVC (deliberately under it — the WAL, the head and compaction share the volume). Doubling the
  series roughly doubles the block growth rate, so the visible effect of keeping every bucket
  would be retention quietly falling from 30 days to under three weeks. Nothing would report
  that; the boards would simply stop going back as far.

  `_count` and `_sum` SURVIVE, so rates and averages are intact for every family — what is lost
  is quantiles, which on a homelab's apiserver nobody acts on. Error rates and request rates are
  the signals that get read, and both come from `_count`.

  etcd_request_duration_seconds_bucket is the one exception, kept deliberately: etcd write
  latency is the early-warning signal the control-plane scrape was justified by, and a quantile
  is the whole point of watching it. It is also, usefully, available from the apiserver's side
  WITHOUT `--etcd-expose-metrics` — so this one family delivers a good part of what the gated
  etcd job would add.
#}
{#
  An EXPLICIT list, not `_bucket` minus an exception. Prometheus matches with RE2, which has no
  lookaround at all — a `(?!etcd_...)` negative-lookahead spelling does not error, it simply
  fails to compile the intent and the rule silently stops dropping anything. Naming the families
  also keeps the list auditable against the measurement above, and makes a new high-cardinality
  family someone's deliberate decision rather than something this rule swallows by accident.
#}
{# NOT `-%}` on the opening tag: it would strip the newline AND the 8-space indent off the first
   body line, landing `metric_relabel_configs:` at column 0 and breaking the YAML. #}
{% macro control_plane_bucket_drop() %}
        metric_relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__name__]
            action: drop
            regex: >-
              (apiserver_request_duration_seconds|apiserver_request_sli_duration_seconds|apiserver_request_body_size_bytes|apiserver_response_sizes|apiserver_watch_list_duration_seconds|apiserver_watch_cache_read_wait_seconds|apiserver_watch_events_sizes|scheduler_plugin_execution_duration_seconds|workqueue_work_duration_seconds|workqueue_queue_duration_seconds|storage_operation_duration_seconds|kubelet_runtime_operations_duration_seconds|rest_client_request_duration_seconds)_bucket
{%- endmacro %}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: prometheus-config
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
data:
  prometheus.yml: |
    global:
      scrape_interval: 15s
    scrape_configs:
      # Prometheus scraping itself. Added in slice 3 B3, and not cosmetic: until now this
      # instance had no self-scrape at all, so its own ingestion rate, TSDB footprint and
      # remote-write receive errors were unreadable — measured 2026-08-07, a query for
      # prometheus_tsdb_head_series returned an empty vector. B5 promotes this instance to
      # monitor-bridge's only Prometheus, so "how full is it and is it keeping up" has to be
      # answerable from inside it before anything depends on it.
      - job_name: prometheus
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['localhost:9090']
      # The collector re-exposes Claude Code's OTLP metrics here. This is the only source of
      # session/token/cost data — nothing else produces it.
      #
      # PER-POD discovery, not the Service, since the DaemonSet conversion (Phase F drain,
      # 2026-08-13): each collector pod exports only the metrics ITS node received, so a
      # Service-target scrape would round-robin between partial views and corrupt every
      # counter. The job names stay — dashboards and monitor-bridge pin them.
      - job_name: otel-collector
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: pod
            namespaces:
              names: [{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}]
        relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_label_app]
            regex: otel-collector
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_container_port_number]
            regex: "8889"
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_node_name]
            target_label: node
      # The collector's OWN metrics, on a different port. These carry the send_failed_*
      # counters that are the only evidence a batch was dropped en route to Loki. Without
      # this scrape a silent export failure is indistinguishable from "Claude Code was idle".
      - job_name: otel-collector-internal
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: pod
            namespaces:
              names: [{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}]
        relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_label_app]
            regex: otel-collector
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_container_port_number]
            regex: "8888"
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_node_name]
            target_label: node
      # Workload readiness for every Pod/Deployment in the cluster (slice 3, D8). This is what
      # gives the seven previously-unmonitorable workloads a health signal at all — see the
      # header of kube-state-metrics.yaml.j2.
      - job_name: kube-state-metrics
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['kube-state-metrics:8080']
      # Slice-6 B2: the CrowdSec engine (LAPI+AppSec) moved in-cluster; its :6060 carries the
      # LAPI/decision metrics the Grafana CrowdSec boards read. The Docker-side scrape that
      # used to cover this now sees only that host's demoted agent.
      - job_name: crowdsec
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['crowdsec.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc:6060']
      # Phase F drain (2026-08-13): the player-stats aggregator moved in-cluster. This
      # job is what re-lit the Apps/player-stats board — the exporter had been scraped
      # by NOTHING since the Docker prometheus retired. Single replica behind the
      # Service, so a static target is unambiguous (unlike the otel DaemonSet jobs).
      - job_name: terraria-stats
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['terraria-stats.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc:9420']
      # Same shape for Valheim (2026-08-13), added with that server's reactivation. Its
      # exporter additionally carries per-player DEATHS, which the Terraria console cannot
      # emit — see roles/k8s/valheim-stats/CLAUDE.md.
      - job_name: valheim-stats
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['valheim-stats.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc:9420']
      # ── Phase D dashboard triage (2026-08-10, PG1/PG2 — slice-7-phase-d-dashboards.md) ──
      # PG1: ported from the Docker prometheus, which scraped these cluster workloads from
      # the wrong side of the migration (through Traefik routes, over the LAN). Same job
      # names as the Docker jobs carried, so every dashboard query and consumer keeps
      # resolving; in-cluster ClusterIP targets, so no route/bypass/DNS in the path.
      # Credentials are file-mounted from the prometheus-scrape-credentials Secret — they
      # must NOT be inlined here (this is a world-readable ConfigMap, not a Secret).
      #
      # Kuma's API key rides HTTP Basic with a blank username; per-monitor up/down,
      # response times and cert-days-remaining. Sole source of monitor_status — the
      # uptime-kuma board and scripts/postflight.py's gates read it.
      - job_name: uptime-kuma
        scrape_interval: 1m
        basic_auth:
          username: ""
          password_file: /etc/prometheus-scrape-creds/kuma_api_key
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['uptime-kuma.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc:3001']
      # HA's prometheus integration; the bearer token is the gate (its route carries no
      # Authelia by design). Feeds hass_* — the home-assistant + ups boards and
      # monitor-bridge's UPS battery checks (which query hass_* unpinned, so the native
      # series feeds them with no origin-label caveat).
      - job_name: home-assistant
        scrape_interval: 1m
        metrics_path: /api/prometheus
        authorization:
          credentials_file: /etc/prometheus-scrape-creds/ha_token
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['home-assistant.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc:8123']
      # PG2: the cluster homelab Loki's own internals (loki_*) — nothing scraped it before
      # this job, despite slice-7-phase-d-loki.md assuming otherwise. Keeps the
      # Logs/loki-internals board alive past the Docker loki's retirement.
      - job_name: loki-homelab
        scrape_interval: 1m
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['loki-homelab.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc:3100']
      # PG2: the k8s edge's traefik_* metrics (exposed since slice-6, scraped by nothing).
      # Via the dedicated traefik-metrics ClusterIP — the internal :8080 entrypoint must
      # not ride the LoadBalancer Service onto the LAN VIP.
      - job_name: traefik-k8s
        scrape_interval: 1m
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['traefik-metrics.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc:8080']
      # ── Phase E2 (2026-08-12): Prometheus consolidation — daniel-server's remaining Docker
      # exporters, scraped directly instead of arriving via that instance's remote_write. Ports
      # are newly LAN-published (prometheus-exporters-lan-firewall.sh locks each to daniel-box
      # only). k8s_bridge_client_ip is daniel-server's address as this play sees it (a literal,
      # not hostvars['daniel-server'] — see its definition: daniel-server isn't guaranteed to be
      # in this play's hostvars).
      #
      # Label parity is load-bearing, not cosmetic: these same job names and an instance of
      # "daniel-server" are what daniel-server's Docker Prometheus produced (directly for `node`
      # via its own metric_relabel_configs; by default target-address for the others — set
      # explicitly here since there's no container DNS name to default from) plus the
      # `origin: daniel-server` external label every one of its remote-written series carried.
      # Reproducing both keeps existing dashboard queries/legends matching unchanged. These five
      # jobs are being removed from daniel-server's prometheus.yml.j2 in this same change, so
      # there is no remaining remote-write source for these job names and no collision risk.
      # Phase F drain (2026-08-14): the Docker node-exporter's LAN-published 9100 retired;
      # the node-exporter DaemonSet is scraped per-pod. Each target keeps an `origin` label
      # carrying its NODE name, so monitor-bridge's origin="daniel-server"-pinned Root Disk
      # / Memory checks keep their exact scope while daniel-box gains node_* it never had.
      # (hostNetwork pods — the pod IP is the node IP.)
      - job_name: node
        scrape_interval: 1m
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: pod
        relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_label_app]
            action: keep
            regex: node-exporter
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_container_port_number]
            action: keep
            regex: "9100"
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_node_name]
            target_label: origin
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_node_name]
            target_label: instance
      # The cadvisor job retired with the Docker cadvisor (2026-08-14, operator decision):
      # its whole purpose was the Docker containers' restart/OOM/throttle series, and those
      # three bridge checks retired with it. Cluster containers' cadvisor metrics come from
      # the kubernetes-cadvisor job below; retargeting the retired checks onto its label
      # shape (container/pod, no `name`) is a Phase G candidate, not a drop-in.
      # Phase F drain (2026-08-14): the Docker promtail's LAN-published 9102 retired with
      # it; the promtail DaemonSet is scraped per-pod like the otel/crowdsec jobs — one
      # target per node, no origin label (it leaves the origin-pinned Scrape Targets set,
      # TARGETS_MIN 3->2, and joins the cluster-native count).
      - job_name: promtail
        scrape_interval: 1m
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: pod
        relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_label_app]
            action: keep
            regex: promtail
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_container_port_number]
            action: keep
            regex: "9080"
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_node_name]
            target_label: node
      # The *arr stack's exportarr sidecars — queue depth, indexer health, per-root-folder free
      # space, and the library counts none of which any other job here carries. Until these
      # landed the entire application layer was up/down only: three roles in the whole estate
      # exposed a metrics port.
      #
      # Discovery by PORT NUMBER, not by an `app` label. The sidecar shares its *arr's pod, so
      # the pod labels say `app: sonarr` and selecting on them would also match the *arr's own
      # container port and scrape an HTML page. 9707 is the sidecar's and nothing else's.
      #
      # `app` is relabelled onto `arr` rather than left alone so a dashboard can group the three
      # without knowing their names, and `container` is dropped: every target is `exportarr`, so
      # the label carries no information and only widens the series.
      - job_name: exportarr
        scrape_interval: 1m
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: pod
        relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_container_port_number]
            action: keep
            regex: "9707"
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_label_app]
            target_label: arr
          # DECIDED: no `origin` label here. `origin="daniel-server"` is the sentinel
          # check_targets counts against TARGETS_MIN=1, and that floor assumes exactly one
          # series: the node job. Labelling the exportarr pods too put three more series
          # behind the same floor, so node-exporter could die and the check would still see
          # >= 1 and read clean. See check.py:385-397, which spells the assumption out.
      # daniel-pi, the one host outside the cluster. STATIC targets, not discovery: the two
      # jobs above use `role: pod`, which by construction only ever finds pods — so the Pi,
      # which runs no kubelet, could never appear in them however many exporters it grew.
      # That is why it contributed zero time series until these two landed.
      #
      # `origin` is set by hand here. The pod-discovery jobs derive it from the node name via
      # relabel; a static target has no such metadata, and without the label the Pi's series
      # would land indistinguishable from the origin-less ones and break _origin_name's
      # "the only host there is" assumption in monitor-bridge.
      #
      # Reached at the LAN IP, not a bridge address: Docker's published-port NAT bypasses the
      # Pi's UFW INPUT chain (documented in initial_setup/tasks/network.yml, where the stale
      # WireGuard allow was removed on exactly this evidence), so no firewall rule is needed
      # and the exporters are LAN-reachable — the same posture glances and dozzle already
      # have on that host.
      - job_name: node-pi
        scrape_interval: 1m
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['{{ k8s_pi_client_ip }}:9100']
            labels:
              origin: daniel-pi
              instance: daniel-pi
      # promtail's OWN metrics, the answer to "is the Pi still shipping logs". A log shipper
      # fails quietly by design — the container stays up, /ready keeps answering, and lines
      # simply stop arriving — so promtail_sent_entries_total and promtail_dropped_entries_total
      # are what make that visible. check_promtail_dropped already reads the second one estate-
      # wide, so this target joins an existing check rather than needing a new one.
      - job_name: promtail-pi
        scrape_interval: 1m
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['{{ k8s_pi_client_ip }}:9080']
            labels:
              origin: daniel-pi
              instance: daniel-pi
      # Phase F drain (2026-08-13): the crowdsec_daniel-server job (the demoted Docker
      # agent's LAN-published 9103) retired with that agent. Its successor is the
      # node-agent DaemonSet — per-POD discovery like the otel jobs, one target per node.
      - job_name: crowdsec-node-agents
        scrape_interval: 1m
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: pod
            namespaces:
              names: [{{ k8s_namespace }}]
        relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_label_app]
            regex: crowdsec-node-agent
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_container_port_number]
            regex: "6060"
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_node_name]
            target_label: node
      # E7 (2026-08-13): the `traefik` job (Docker edge metrics on 9104) retired with the
      # edge. The bare `traefik` job name retires with it — the cluster edge stays
      # `traefik-k8s`, and monitor-bridge's traefik/cert checks are unpinned so they
      # now read that edge's series alone.
      # First Longhorn scrape (2026-08-13): volume robustness/fullness and node storage
      # headroom have had no series at all until now. Check-arm wiring comes later.
      #
      # It then had none for a second reason (2026-08-15 to 2026-08-16): Longhorn v1.12 ships
      # NetworkPolicies that admit only its own components, so this target read `up == 0` with a
      # connection refused and nothing noticed, because the disclosure above reads the same
      # whether the scrape is unwired or fenced off. Reopened by
      # roles/setup/k3s/files/longhorn-metrics-networkpolicy.yaml.
      #
      # FIXED (2026-08-17): this used to be a static_configs target against the
      # longhorn-backend ClusterIP. Prometheus held one keep-alive connection to whichever of
      # the two longhorn-manager pods the Service happened to route it to for hours at a time,
      # so `count by (node) (longhorn_disk_usage_bytes)` showed daniel-server only — verified
      # live: 0 samples for daniel-box over a 1h window vs 60/60 for daniel-server, and 7-day
      # totals of ~600 vs ~3102 (17% vs 31%). It only swapped endpoints on a manager pod
      # restart, so daniel-box's Longhorn disk headroom was genuinely unobservable even though
      # `nodes.longhorn.io daniel-box` shows a real, Ready, Schedulable disk carrying dozens of
      # replicas — this was a scrape defect, not a disk actually missing from the cluster.
      #
      # Now uses kubernetes_sd_configs with role: endpoints, scoped to longhorn-system, so both
      # longhorn-manager pods behind the Service are scraped independently every interval.
      # Chose role: endpoints over role: endpointslice because the live Endpoints object
      # already carries a clean, populated targetRef for both addresses (`kubectl get
      # endpoints -n longhorn-system longhorn-backend -o yaml` showed targetRef.name
      # longhorn-manager-ntbrt / longhorn-manager-ltwpr for the daniel-box/daniel-server
      # addresses respectively — confirmed against the equivalent EndpointSlice, which carries
      # the same targetRef), so __meta_kubernetes_endpoint_address_target_name resolves
      # reliably here despite longhorn-manager being DaemonSet-managed. RBAC already grants
      # `get, list, watch` on `endpoints` cluster-wide (ClusterRole below), so this collects
      # without any RBAC change. No dashboard or check.py consumer pins the old ClusterIP
      # `instance` label (grepped both), so changing `instance` to each pod's IP is safe.
      #
      # Expect longhorn_manager_* and longhorn_instance_manager_* series counts to roughly
      # double: today's blended-from-two-pods series becomes two real per-pod series. That's
      # the fix working, not a regression.
      #
      # Next step (not done here): a Longhorn storage dashboard + check-arm, once this fix has
      # been deployed long enough to have real data. Two traps for whoever builds it:
      # longhorn_volume_last_backup_at has 14 series that all read 0 (a freshness panel on it
      # would falsely report "no backups ever"), and longhorn_volume_robustness is one-hot over
      # a `state` label (healthy/degraded/faulted/unknown x 14 volumes = 56 series) rather than
      # an attached/detached split, so any panel must select on `state` explicitly.
      - job_name: longhorn
        scrape_interval: 1m
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: endpoints
            namespaces:
              names: [longhorn-system]
        relabel_configs:
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_service_name]
            regex: longhorn-backend
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_endpoint_port_name]
            regex: manager
            action: keep
          - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_endpoint_address_target_name]
            target_label: pod
      # This stack's OWN Loki/Tempo internals, distinct from the loki-homelab job above
      # (that one is the general-purpose homelab log store). instance_role separates the
      # two so a dashboard/alert query doesn't accidentally blend claude-otel's internal
      # health series with the homelab's.
      - job_name: loki-internal
        scrape_interval: 1m
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['loki.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}.svc:3100']
            labels:
              instance_role: observability
      - job_name: tempo-internal
        scrape_interval: 1m
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['tempo.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}.svc:3200']
            labels:
              instance_role: observability
      # Per-pod CPU/memory from each kubelet's cAdvisor, dialed directly at the kubelet's
      # :10250 rather than through the API server's /proxy/ path. `role: node` discovery
      # already defaults __address__ to the node's kubelet endpoint (host:10250), so no
      # __address__ relabel is needed — only the scheme, path and auth.
      #
      # The direct path narrows the RBAC this needs: the kubelet's own authorizer maps
      # /metrics/cadvisor (and /metrics, /metrics/resource) to the `nodes/metrics` subresource
      # rather than `nodes/proxy` — same as when reached via the API server proxy, since the
      # kubelet applies that mapping regardless of how the request arrived. `nodes/proxy` was
      # only needed for the old /api/v1/nodes/<name>/proxy/... path and is dropped from the
      # ClusterRole below now that nothing uses it.
      #
      # insecure_skip_verify is on the kubelet hop: k3s's kubelet serving cert doesn't carry a
      # SAN Prometheus can validate against here. The bearer token is still verified by the API
      # server via TokenReview, so this weakens transport identity, not authn.
      - job_name: kubernetes-cadvisor
        scheme: https
        metrics_path: /metrics/cadvisor
        tls_config:
          ca_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt
          insecure_skip_verify: true
        bearer_token_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: node
        relabel_configs:
          - action: labelmap
            regex: __meta_kubernetes_node_label_(.+)
      # The kubelet's OWN metrics, distinct from the cadvisor job above. cadvisor answers "what
      # is each container doing"; these answer "is the kubelet itself healthy" — pod start
      # duration, PLEG relist latency, eviction counts, volume mount failures. A node that is
      # slow to start pods or is quietly evicting them reads as a perfectly Ready node until
      # one of these series is available to look at.
      #
      # Same host, same auth, same RBAC as cadvisor: the kubelet authorizer maps /metrics and
      # /metrics/resource onto `nodes/metrics` exactly as it maps /metrics/cadvisor, so this
      # needs no new grant. insecure_skip_verify is on for the reason stated above.
      - job_name: kubernetes-kubelet
        scheme: https
        metrics_path: /metrics
        tls_config:
          ca_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt
          insecure_skip_verify: true
        bearer_token_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: node
        relabel_configs:
          - action: labelmap
            regex: __meta_kubernetes_node_label_(.+)
{{ control_plane_bucket_drop() }}
      # Per-pod CPU/memory straight from the kubelet's summary API rather than from cadvisor's
      # cgroup walk. Cheaper and lower-cardinality than the cadvisor series, and it is what
      # `kubectl top` reads — so a dashboard built on it agrees with the CLI, where one built
      # on cadvisor's container_memory_working_set_bytes does not always.
      - job_name: kubernetes-kubelet-resource
        scheme: https
        metrics_path: /metrics/resource
        tls_config:
          ca_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt
          insecure_skip_verify: true
        bearer_token_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: node
        relabel_configs:
          - action: labelmap
            regex: __meta_kubernetes_node_label_(.+)
      # The API server's own metrics — request latency by verb and resource, error rates,
      # in-flight request saturation, and the etcd request duration the apiserver observes from
      # its side. This is the layer between "a pod is unhealthy" and "the cluster is unhealthy",
      # and nothing else in this config can see it.
      #
      # RBAC note, and the trap: /metrics on the API SERVER is a non-resource URL, so the
      # `nodes/metrics` grant that covers the three kubelet jobs above does NOT cover this one.
      # It needs the explicit `nonResourceURLs` rule added to the ClusterRole below. Without it
      # the scrape 403s while every other job stays green — a partial failure that reads as a
      # working config until you look at the target list.
      #
      # No insecure_skip_verify here: unlike the kubelet, the API server's serving cert carries
      # a SAN for kubernetes.default.svc, so the in-cluster CA validates it properly.
      - job_name: kubernetes-apiserver
        scheme: https
        metrics_path: /metrics
        tls_config:
          ca_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt
        bearer_token_file: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['kubernetes.default.svc:443']
{{ control_plane_bucket_drop() }}
{% if k3s_etcd_expose_metrics | default(false) %}
      # Embedded etcd, the single existential risk on a one-server control plane: db size
      # against its quota, fsync/commit latency, leader changes, and proposal failures. None of
      # it is visible anywhere else, and the failure it warns about is the one the restore
      # runbook exists for.
      #
      # GATED, because the target does not exist until k3s is restarted with
      # --etcd-expose-metrics. k3s serves this on 2381 either way; the flag only moves the bind
      # from 127.0.0.1 to 0.0.0.0, which is what puts it in reach of a pod-network scrape. Plain
      # HTTP and no auth by k3s's design — the port is protected the same way :10250 is, by the
      # host's default-deny UFW policy, and it carries no cluster data, only counters.
      #
      # ONE switch drives both halves — `k3s_etcd_expose_metrics` in group_vars/all.yml gates
      # this job and the k3s flag alike, so they cannot drift into "port open, nobody scraping"
      # or "job armed, port closed". They still land in two runs: flip the var, run
      # k3s-bringup.yml to rewrite the unit and restart k3s, THEN deploy claude-otel. Deploying
      # this first only means the target reads down until the bringup run catches up.
      - job_name: etcd
        scrape_interval: 30s
        static_configs:
          - targets: ['{{ server_ip }}:2381']
            labels:
              instance_role: control-plane
{% endif %}
---
# Prometheus authenticates to the API server as itself for the kubernetes_sd_configs discovery
# above (enumerating nodes for scrape targets), and its bearer token is presented to each
# kubelet directly for the cadvisor scrape. Read-only and list/watch-only. It cannot mutate
# anything, and it is deliberately NOT granted secrets or configmaps.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: prometheus
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
automountServiceAccountToken: true
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: prometheus
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: [nodes, nodes/metrics, services, endpoints, pods]
    verbs: [get, list, watch]
  # The API server's /metrics is a NON-RESOURCE URL — it is not a `resources:` entry and the
  # `nodes/metrics` grant above does not reach it. Without this rule the kubernetes-apiserver
  # job 403s on every scrape while the three kubelet jobs stay green, so the config reads as
  # working. `get` on this one path only: it grants no read of any object.
  - nonResourceURLs: ["/metrics"]
    verbs: [get]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: prometheus
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: prometheus
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: prometheus
    namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: prometheus-data
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: {{ claude_otel_storage_class }}
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: {{ claude_otel_prometheus_storage }}
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: prometheus
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  replicas: 1
  strategy:
    type: Recreate  # ReadWriteOnce PVC — see the note on the loki Deployment
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: prometheus
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: prometheus
        netpol-baseline: enforced
    spec:
      # Without this the pod runs as the namespace `default` SA, whose token cannot list nodes —
      # kubernetes_sd_configs then discovers zero targets and the cadvisor job silently reports
      # no series rather than erroring.
      serviceAccountName: prometheus
      enableServiceLinks: false
      securityContext:
        runAsUser: 65534
        runAsGroup: 65534
        fsGroup: 65534
      containers:
        - name: prometheus
          image: {{ claude_otel_prometheus_image }}
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
              drop: [ALL]
          args:
            - --config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
            - --storage.tsdb.path=/prometheus
            - --storage.tsdb.retention.time={{ claude_otel_prometheus_retention }}
            # A SECOND cap, on bytes. The time cap alone does not bound the PVC: retention.time
            # says how old a block may get, not how much a block holds, so an ingest-rate rise
            # grows the footprint without aging anything out. A full Prometheus PVC does not
            # degrade, it wedges — and the instance that would alert on it is this one. Whichever
            # cap binds first wins, which is the intended behaviour. (The rise this was sized for
            # was daniel-server remote-writing its TSDB here; that stopped at Phase E2/F, and the
            # receiver came out on 2026-08-16 — the cap is still wanted, the rationale moved on.)
            - --storage.tsdb.retention.size={{ claude_otel_prometheus_retention_size }}
            # NO --web.enable-remote-write-receiver. It accepted the remote-write protocol on
            # /api/v1/write for daniel-server's Docker Prometheus (slice 3, B3); that sender was
            # retired in Phase E2 and daniel-server has had no Docker at all since 2026-08-14.
            # The ingress route onto it was deleted on 2026-08-16 as dead surface, but the flag
            # kept the door open on the ClusterIP, where nothing fences it: `observability` has
            # no NetworkPolicy, so any pod in the cluster could push arbitrary series in. Turning
            # the receiver off closes that with certainty; a policy would only narrow it.
          ports:
            - name: http
              containerPort: 9090
              # On the node's loopback so otelq can reach it; see
              # claude_otel_query_host_ip in defaults/main.yml.
              hostPort: 9090
              hostIP: {{ claude_otel_query_host_ip }}
          volumeMounts:
            - name: prometheus-config
              mountPath: /etc/prometheus
            - name: prometheus-data
              mountPath: /prometheus
            # /etc, not /run/secrets (the ServiceAccount-token-shadow guard); 0444 because
            # prometheus runs as nobody and secret-volume files are root-owned.
            - name: prometheus-scrape-creds
              mountPath: /etc/prometheus-scrape-creds
              readOnly: true
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /-/ready
              port: 9090
            initialDelaySeconds: 15
            periodSeconds: 10
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 50m
              memory: 128Mi
            limits:
              cpu: "1"
              memory: 1Gi
      volumes:
        - name: prometheus-config
          configMap:
            name: prometheus-config
        - name: prometheus-data
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: prometheus-data
        - name: prometheus-scrape-creds
          secret:
            secretName: prometheus-scrape-credentials
            defaultMode: 0o444
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: prometheus
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  # Pinned: host-side query tools carry this address as a constant. See
  # claude_otel_prometheus_cluster_ip in defaults/main.yml for why.
  clusterIP: {{ claude_otel_prometheus_cluster_ip }}
  selector:
    app: prometheus
  ports:
    - name: http
      port: 9090
      targetPort: 9090
